"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"][3]={"school_computer_science_en:M3-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M3-L1 – Variables, values and data types<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming algorithms<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary (core)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3 – Characters, Unicode and file\nformats<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Write a reproducible Python\nprogram with assignments, types and output.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A school travel app shows a distance of 1.25 km. A map of the same\nroute uses metres. The wrong units can turn a correct calculation into\nan incorrect display. We will write a short program that keeps the input\nand result separate and makes the conversion easy to check.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Names, values and types<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>assignment<\/strong> with <code>=<\/code> binds a name to a\nvalue. Python evaluates the right-hand side first. In\n<code>distance_m = distance_km * 1000<\/code>, Python reads the value\nbound to <code>distance_km<\/code>, multiplies it by 1000 and binds the\nnew name to the result. The names indicate the units. To Python they are\nordinary names with no built-in conversion rule.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>int<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>1000<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Integer<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>float<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>1.25<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Floating-point number<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>str<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>\"km\"<\/code><\/td>\n<td>String<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bool<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>True<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Boolean value, with <code>False<\/code> as the alternative<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The type belongs to the value and affects which operations are\npossible. <code>+<\/code> adds two numbers and joins two strings. A\nstring containing digits such as <code>\"1.25\"<\/code> can be converted\nexplicitly to a floating-point number using <code>float(\"1.25\")<\/code>.\nPython uses a point as the decimal separator in numeric literals.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000466_en.svg\"\nalt=\"distance_km refers to 1.25 of type float, metres_per_km to 1000 of type int, distance_m to 1250.0 of type float, and unit to the string m.\" />\n<figcaption>The same assignment rules bind names to numbers and\nstrings.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Converting kilometres to metres<\/h2>\n<p>Save the block as <code>distance.py<\/code> and run it with\n<code>python distance.py<\/code> or <code>python3 distance.py<\/code>,\ndepending on the name of your Python 3 command. The block needs no extra\npackages or input. <code>print<\/code> writes the value and unit\nseparated by a space.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>distance_km <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.25<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>metres_per_km <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>distance_m <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> distance_km <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> metres_per_km<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>unit <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;m&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(distance_m, unit)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> distance_m <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1250.0<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The output is <code>1250.0 m<\/code>. The calculation uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.25<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1250<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.25 \\cdot 1000 = 1250<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe result has type <code>float<\/code>, so Python prints the decimal\nplace here. The <code>assert<\/code> line checks equality with the\nexpected numeric value. When the condition holds, <code>assert<\/code>\nproduces no output.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Change <code>distance_km<\/code> to <code>0.75<\/code>. What output\nand expected value are needed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A distance arrives as the text <code>\"1.25\"<\/code>. What happens\nwhen it is multiplied by <code>1000<\/code>? How can you calculate the\ndistance instead?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>750<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.75 \\cdot 1000 = 750<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe output is <code>750.0 m<\/code>. Change the check to\n<code>assert distance_m == 750.0<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Multiplication repeats the string 1000 times.\n<code>float(\"1.25\") * 1000<\/code> uses the numeric value and gives\n<code>1250.0<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common mistakes and limits<\/h2>\n<p><code>distance_km = 1,25<\/code> produces a pair of values in Python,\nnamely <code>(1, 25)<\/code>. Write <code>1.25<\/code> in your program.\n<code>\"1.25\" + 1<\/code> raises a <code>TypeError<\/code> because Python\ndoes not define addition for this combination of types. Many decimal\nnumbers have only approximate representations as <code>float<\/code>\nvalues. The chosen values <code>1.25<\/code> and <code>0.75<\/code> can be\nrepresented exactly in binary. Later comparisons of measurements may\nrequire tolerances justified by the application.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>An assignment gives a value a name. The value determines which\noperations are allowed. Meaningful names, a visible unit and a known\ntest value make a conversion easy to trace.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2 – Conditions and\nbranches<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/FA/IBS/user_upload/GI-Bildungsstandards_Informatik_Sek_I__2025__-_blau.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §5.2 · Write a reproducible\nPython program with assignments, types and output. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.13/tutorial/introduction.html\">Python\nSoftware Foundation – Python Tutorial – An Informal Introduction to\nPython<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.1 · Write a reproducible Python\nprogram with assignments, types and output. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22\n· Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M3-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M3-L2 – Conditions and branches<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming algorithms<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary (core)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1 – Variables, values and data\ntypes<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Use comparisons and Boolean\nexpressions to choose between cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>The box office for a school play has a count of available seats. With\nzero seats the display should say “full”. With one or two seats it\nshould say “few”. From three seats onwards it should say “available”.\nThe count can only be a non-negative integer. How can we translate all\nthree cases into a program without leaving a gap?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conditions ask questions about a value<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>comparison<\/strong> produces a Boolean value.\n<code>seats == 0<\/code> checks equality. <code>seats &lt; 3<\/code> asks\nwhether the value is below 3. In Python <code>=<\/code> assigns a value\nand <code>==<\/code> checks equality. With <code>and<\/code> both parts\nmust be true. For <code>or<\/code> one true part is enough.\n<code>not<\/code> reverses a Boolean value.<\/p>\n<p><code>if<\/code> checks the first condition. Python checks an\n<code>elif<\/code> only if no earlier branch was selected. If no\ncondition holds, an <code>else<\/code> branch is executed when present. A\nline ending with a colon starts the branch. Lines with the same\nindentation belong to that branch, using four spaces here.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000428_en.svg\"\nalt=\"If no seats are available, the result is full. Otherwise a check for fewer than three leads to few if true and available if false.\" />\n<figcaption>The second question is reached only when the first condition\nis false.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Displaying seat availability<\/h2>\n<p>Start with two free seats. <code>seats == 0<\/code> is false.\n<code>seats &lt; 3<\/code> is true. The second assignment sets the\ndisplay value. The final <code>print<\/code> is outside the conditional\nstatement, so the other inputs reach it too.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>seats <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> seats <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    status <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;full&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">elif<\/span> seats <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    status <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;few&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    status <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;available&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(status)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> status <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;few&quot;<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Available seats<\/th>\n<th><code>seats == 0<\/code><\/th>\n<th><code>seats &lt; 3<\/code> check<\/th>\n<th>Output<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>true<\/td>\n<td>skipped<\/td>\n<td><code>full<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>false<\/td>\n<td>true<\/td>\n<td><code>few<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>false<\/td>\n<td>false<\/td>\n<td><code>available<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The output strings are labels for the three cases. Because the first\ntest already handles zero, the second test does not need to repeat the\nlower bound of 1 here. As a standalone question,\n<code>seats &gt;= 1 and seats &lt; 3<\/code> would be more precise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Trace the inputs 0, 1, 2 and 3. For each value, identify which\nbranch is chosen.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A student tests <code>seats &lt; 3<\/code> first and tests for zero\nin the <code>elif<\/code>. Which input shows a difference?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add a first condition that displays an error for negative counts.\nExplain why the condition belongs at the start.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">0 takes the <code>if<\/code> branch and gives <code>full<\/code>. 1\nand 2 take the <code>elif<\/code> branch and give <code>few<\/code>. 3\ntakes the <code>else<\/code> branch and gives\n<code>available<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For 0, <code>seats &lt; 3<\/code> is true. Python immediately selects\n<code>few<\/code> and skips the other branch.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Check for an invalid count with <code>if seats &lt; 0:<\/code> and\nmake the previous first test <code>elif seats == 0:<\/code>. Otherwise, a\nvalue such as −1 would be treated as “few”.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common mistakes and limits<\/h2>\n<p>A second independent <code>if<\/code> can be true even after the first\none has run. An <code>if<\/code>, <code>elif<\/code> and <code>else<\/code>\nchain runs at most one branch. The table deliberately includes values\nnear the boundaries. Testing with 10 seats alone would miss a fault that\noccurs at exactly 3. Text values such as <code>\"3\"<\/code> must be read\nseparately and converted to an appropriate numeric type before numeric\ncomparisons.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Write the rule in words first and check its boundaries. Comparisons\nproduce Boolean values. The order of the conditions determines which\nbranch runs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3 – Loops and tracing<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/FA/IBS/user_upload/GI-Bildungsstandards_Informatik_Sek_I__2025__-_blau.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §5.2 · Use comparisons and\nBoolean expressions to choose between cases. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22\n· Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.13/tutorial/controlflow.html\">Python\nSoftware Foundation – Python Tutorial – More Control Flow Tools<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: §4.1 · Use comparisons and Boolean expressions to\nchoose between cases. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified:\n2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M3-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M3-L3 – Loops and tracing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming algorithms<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary (core)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2 – Conditions and branches<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Trace iteration using for,\nwhile, state changes and stopping conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A collection station fills three containers with 2 litres of water\neach. After every container, the amount filled so far is recorded. We\ncould write out three almost identical instructions. A loop describes\nthe same action once and makes it clear when it repeats.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Repetition and progress through states<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>for loop<\/strong> in Python goes through the elements of a\nsequence. <code>range(1, 4)<\/code> yields 1, 2 and 3 in order. It\nincludes the lower bound and excludes the upper bound. The loop sets the\nvalue of <code>cup<\/code> on each iteration.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>while loop<\/strong> checks its condition before each\niteration. If it is false on the first check, the loop body does not run\nat all. A counting loop needs an initial value and a change that moves\nit towards the stopping point. A <strong>trace table<\/strong> records\nthe relevant values after each iteration. It helps spot missing updates\nand off-by-one errors.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000443_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The condition is true at counter values 1, 2 and 3. The amount after those iterations rises to 2, 4 and 6 litres. At counter value 4 the condition fails and the amount stays at 6 litres.\" />\n<figcaption>The last row records the stopping check without another\niteration.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recording three containers<\/h2>\n<p>The initial value of <code>total_l<\/code> is 0. For\n<code>total_l = total_l + 2<\/code>, Python reads the previous value,\nadds 2 and assigns the result back. The variable accumulates the partial\namounts. In the <code>while<\/code> block, the counter starts at 1 and\nincreases by 1 after every iteration. At 4 the condition\n<code>cup &lt;= 3<\/code> is false. Both loops produce the pairs (1, 2),\n(2, 4) and (3, 6).<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>total_l <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> cup <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    total_l <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> total_l <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(cup, total_l)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> total_l <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>total_l <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cup <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> cup <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    total_l <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> total_l <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(cup, total_l)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    cup <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> cup <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> cup <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> total_l <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>1 2\n2 4\n3 6\n1 2\n2 4\n3 6<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Make a trace table using <code>range(1, 5)<\/code> with the same\namount of 2 litres per container. List all running totals.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A version starts with <code>cup = 1<\/code> and uses\n<code>while cup &lt; 3:<\/code>. What is missing from the output? How\nwould you change the condition?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens with <code>while cup &lt;= 3:<\/code> if the loop body\nnever changes <code>cup<\/code>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The values for <code>cup<\/code> are 1, 2, 3 and 4. The corresponding\ntotals are 2, 4, 6 and 8 litres.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The third iteration with 6 litres is missing because\n<code>3 &lt; 3<\/code> is false. With <code>cup &lt;= 3<\/code> the third\niteration is included.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>cup<\/code> stays at 1 and <code>cup &lt;= 3<\/code> remains\ntrue. Under these assumptions the loop does not stop on its own.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common mistakes and limits<\/h2>\n<p>The counter and the running total serve different purposes. Setting\nthe counter to <code>total_l<\/code> would skip containers. When the\nnumber of iterations is known, <code>for<\/code> with <code>range<\/code>\nsupplies the counter values. <code>while<\/code> is suitable when a\ncondition is the natural focus of the problem. If progress is unclear,\ncheck on paper whether the possible iterations keep moving towards a\npoint where the condition becomes false.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A <code>for<\/code> loop goes through values, while a\n<code>while<\/code> loop checks a condition. A short trace table shows\nintermediate states, the last iteration actually executed and the final\nvalue.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4 – Lists, functions and\ndecomposition<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/FA/IBS/user_upload/GI-Bildungsstandards_Informatik_Sek_I__2025__-_blau.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §5.2 · Trace iteration using\nfor, while, state changes and stopping conditions. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.13/tutorial/controlflow.html\">Python\nSoftware Foundation – Python Tutorial – More Control Flow Tools<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: §§4.2–4.4 · Trace iteration using for, while, state\nchanges and stopping conditions. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M3-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M3-L4 – Lists, functions and decomposition<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming algorithms<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary (core)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3 – Loops and tracing<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Process a list through smaller\nfunctions with return values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A school weather club records a set of readings. For one report, only\nreadings of 8 or more are relevant. The report should show the mean of\nthat selection. If the log contains no qualifying values, it should show\nno apparent mean. We can separate selection from evaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lists and manageable subtasks<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>list<\/strong> stores values in order and can be empty. The\nfirst value of <code>[8, 4, 9, 6]<\/code> is at index <code>0<\/code>.\nWith <code>for reading in readings<\/code> we can read the values in\nsequence. <code>append<\/code> adds a value to the end of a list. When\nselecting values, we build a new list while iterating over the\noriginal.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>function<\/strong> takes arguments when called and provides\na result through <code>return<\/code>. In\n<code>def keep_high(readings, floor):<\/code>, <code>readings<\/code> and\n<code>floor<\/code> are the parameter names. They refer to the values\npassed at the call. We give each function a clear purpose.\n<code>keep_high<\/code> retains every value at or above a lower bound in\nits original order. <code>mean_or_none<\/code> computes the arithmetic\nmean of a non-empty list and returns <code>None<\/code> for an empty\nlist. Here <code>None<\/code> represents the absence of a computable\nresult.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000442_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The list 8, 4, 9, 6 is filtered with a lower bound of 8. The resulting list 8, 9 goes to the second function, which returns 8.5 as the mean.\" />\n<figcaption>The first return value becomes the input of the second\nfunction.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connecting two functions<\/h2>\n<p>The values 8 and 9 satisfy <code>reading &gt;= floor<\/code>. The\nvalues 4 and 6 are left out. The mean of the selection is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8+9)/2=8.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second function uses <code>sum<\/code> for the total and\n<code>len<\/code> for the number of values.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> keep_high(readings, floor):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    selected <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> reading <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> readings:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> reading <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> floor:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            selected.append(reading)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> selected<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> mean_or_none(readings):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(readings) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(readings) <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(readings)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>readings <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>selected <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> keep_high(readings, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>mean <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mean_or_none(selected)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(selected)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(mean)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> selected <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> mean <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">8.5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> readings <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> mean_or_none(keep_high(readings, <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The output is <code>[8, 9]<\/code> followed by <code>8.5<\/code> on the\nnext line. The last check sets the lower bound to 10. The selection is\nempty and the second function returns <code>None<\/code>.\n<code>is None<\/code> tests for that exact special Python value.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which values are retained when the lower bound is 6? Calculate their\nmean.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does each function produce for an empty input list?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why would <code>return 0<\/code> be ambiguous for an empty list?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The result is <code>[8, 9, 6]<\/code> in that order. Their mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>23<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">23/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about 7.67.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>keep_high([], 8)<\/code> returns <code>[]<\/code>.\n<code>mean_or_none([])<\/code> returns <code>None<\/code>. Division by\nzero is avoided.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The mean of <code>[-1, 1]<\/code> actually is <code>0.0<\/code>.\n<code>None<\/code> allows a missing mean to be distinguished from a\ncalculated value of zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common mistakes and limits<\/h2>\n<p><code>print(selected)<\/code> shows the list.\n<code>return selected<\/code> provides it to the calling code for further\ncalculation. <code>append<\/code> modifies the list directly. Therefore\n<code>selected = selected.append(reading)<\/code> rebinds the name to the\nreturn value <code>None<\/code>. The functions assume the list contains\nnumbers. Text or missing readings need an input rule before they can be\nevaluated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Lists hold ordered data. Separate functions let you test the\nselection independently of the mean calculation. Handling an empty list\nexplicitly gives the result a clear meaning.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5 – Test cases and\ndebugging<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/FA/IBS/user_upload/GI-Bildungsstandards_Informatik_Sek_I__2025__-_blau.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §5.1 · Process a list through\nsmaller functions with return values. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.13/tutorial/controlflow.html\">Python\nSoftware Foundation – Python Tutorial – More Control Flow Tools<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: §4.8 · Process a list through smaller functions with\nreturn values. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified:\n2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.13/tutorial/datastructures.html\">Python\nSoftware Foundation – Python Tutorial – Data Structures<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson\nreference: §5.1 · Process a list through smaller functions with return\nvalues. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M3-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M3-L5 – Test cases and debugging<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming algorithms<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary (core)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4 – Lists, functions and\ndecomposition<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Test normal, boundary and\ninvalid cases and isolate a defect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A group room has space for at most ten people. A booking program\naccepts a group of eight but rejects a group of exactly ten. The program\nruns without crashing. Its result is wrong because one part of the\nrequirement was translated into the wrong comparison.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Expected results, comparisons and causes<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>test case<\/strong> pairs an input with an expected result.\nWrite the requirement first. For a non-negative integer group size, the\nfunction returns <code>True<\/code> up to and including 10 and\n<code>False<\/code> for larger values. A negative group size is outside\nthe agreed range and raises <code>ValueError<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>normal case<\/strong> checks typical behaviour, such as a\ngroup of 6. A <strong>boundary case<\/strong> tests the edge of a range\nor the values immediately next to it, here 10 and 11. An <strong>error\ncase<\/strong> checks the response to invalid input. A <strong>regression\ntest<\/strong> keeps a fixed defect covered by a permanent test case, so\nit can be detected again after later changes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000463_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A group of 6 expects True. The boundary cases 10 and 11 expect True and False, respectively. The invalid input minus 1 expects ValueError.\" />\n<figcaption>Among these inputs, only the test with 10 detects the\nincorrect comparison &lt; 10.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducing and fixing a defect<\/h2>\n<p>The incorrect return statement is <code>return people &lt; 10<\/code>.\nWith <code>people = 10<\/code> it gives <code>False<\/code> even though\nthe room has space. Trace the same comparison for 6 and 11. Both results\nhappen to match the requirement. The value 10 narrows down the cause.\nCorrect the comparison to <code>people &lt;= 10<\/code>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> group_fits(people):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> people <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;group size must be non-negative&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> people <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> group_fits(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> group_fits(<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> group_fits(<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">try<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    group_fits(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">except<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">pass<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">AssertionError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;expected ValueError for a negative group size&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><code>raise<\/code> deliberately reports an exception.\n<code>try<\/code> runs the negative-input test. If\n<code>ValueError<\/code> occurs, that is the expected behaviour and\n<code>pass<\/code> has no effect. If no exception occurs,\n<code>else<\/code> reports the failing test by raising\n<code>AssertionError<\/code>. If all checks pass, this program produces\nno output.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which test fails if you use <code>&lt; 10<\/code> again? Why is a\ntest with 11 insufficient?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add a normal case above the boundary and a test for the lowest valid\ninput in the specification.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The function mistakenly returns <code>False<\/code> for every\nnegative group size. Which test reports the mistake?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>assert group_fits(10) is True<\/code> fails. Both\n<code>11 &lt; 10<\/code> and <code>11 &lt;= 10<\/code> give\n<code>False<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Use, for example, <code>assert group_fits(20) is False<\/code> and\n<code>assert group_fits(0) is True<\/code>. Under the agreed rule, 0 is\nvalid.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>group_fits(-1)<\/code> does not raise the expected exception.\nThe <code>else<\/code> branch therefore raises\n<code>AssertionError<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common mistakes and limits<\/h2>\n<p>Derive the expected result from the rule. If the test uses the same\ncomparison as the program to calculate that result, it may miss a\nmistake that both share. The checks cover selected cases. They do not\nprove correctness for all inputs. The function assumes integers and does\nnot validate text or other types here. Use <code>assert<\/code> for these\nlearning checks. Python can disable them with <code>-O<\/code>, so the\nrequired input validation is a separate branch inside the function\nitself.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Record which inputs are valid and what they should produce. A test\nright at the boundary helps locate a defect. Keep it as a regression\ntest after making the correction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1 – Networks, packets and\nprotocols<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/FA/IBS/user_upload/GI-Bildungsstandards_Informatik_Sek_I__2025__-_blau.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §5.2 · Test normal, boundary and\ninvalid cases and isolate a defect. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/unittest.html\">Python\nSoftware Foundation – unittest – Unit testing framework<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson\nreference: Basic example · Test normal, boundary and invalid cases and\nisolate a defect. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified:\n2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
